research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348095-+{"id":"ex-integral-test-applied-to-the-p-series","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_series.html","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_impropriemann.html"],"rationale":"The sources give the p-series threshold p>1 and the integral test. The item specializes to rational powers available in the library, indexes f_p(t)=(t+1)^{-p} from zero, and expresses improper convergence as boundedness of its proper-integral sequence. The mathematical threshold is sourced, but its last p=1 sentence falsely denies existence of a primitive and is proposed for repair.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348096-+{"id":"ex-two-root-x-and-its-unbounded-derivative","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www2.hawaii.edu/~gradysw/apex/sec_derivative.html","https://faculty.gvsu.edu/boelkinm/Home/ACS/sec-6-5-improper.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that sqrt(x) is continuous, has derivative 1/(2sqrt(x)) for x>0, is not differentiable at zero, and has an unbounded derivative near zero. The item scales by 2 and packages those facts to explain why the proper Riemann FTC does not apply on [0,1].","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348097-+{"id":"cex-an-integrable-function-with-no-primitive","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_ftc.html","https://math.colgate.edu/math323/dlantz/extras/notesC5.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources give the sign function's integral function as an absolute-value function and Darboux's theorem that derivatives cannot jump. The item packages these standard facts as an explicit refutation of the claim that every Riemann-integrable function has a primitive.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log:1348098:+{"id":"cex-a-function-with-a-primitive-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"none","urls":[],"rationale":"The exact disjoint-interval quartic bump construction is a bespoke polynomial replacement for the classical trigonometric witness. The proof checks seam derivatives, differentiability at the accumulation point, and the displayed unbounded derivative values directly. No semantically identical source for this construction surfaced, and its deliberate coefficient and interval design supports the positive local-invention determination.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348099-+{"id":"cex-a-composition-of-integrable-functions-that-is-not-integrable","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00591-022-00318-x"],"rationale":"The peer-reviewed source gives the same counterexample: Thomae's Riemann-integrable function composed with the integrable indicator of the positive range becomes Dirichlet's nonintegrable function. The repository's discontinuity-set verification is local.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
research/audit/wave11-dispatch/audit-beta-a1a2.log-1348100-+{"id":"cex-absolute-value-integrable-without-the-function","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"The signed Dirichlet function, equal to 1 on rationals and -1 on irrationals, is a standard counterexample: its absolute value is constant while every Darboux lower and upper sum differs. Search located the exact witness only in non-qualifying discussion sources; reputable texts located during the pass state Dirichlet nonintegrability but not this full signed claim. It therefore requires Alpha's independent established-knowledge concurrence and is not mislabeled as invented.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-09"}
